  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Only significant change on this update has been\nto cancel the flood watch early as QPF trends continue to be on\nthe decrease and with the more progressive nature of shower and\nthunderstorm activity the flooding risk continues to decrease.\n\nPrev Disc...Latest mid level water vapor imagery shows an upper\nlevel trough currently stalling across the Ohio River Valley \nand into the Tennessee River Valley and this will be our main \nweather maker for the next few days. This will keep S to SW'rly \nflow aloft which will continue to advect in ample moisture from \nthe Gulf and this will also result in several mid level \nshortwaves transiting around the base of this trough and across \nthe Carolinas. A shortwave is tracking across the area this \nmorning with yet another forecast to track across the area on \nTue. At the surface, closed low looks to be over the Great Lakes\nregion as it slowly shifts NC and becomes separated from the \nparent trough. \n\nHere in ENC, a narrow, scattered line of convection with brief \nheavy downpours is moving through the region along the\naforementioned shortwave, with stratiform rain behind it. In \nthe next 6 hours, This scattered band should push offshore with \nadditional convection near Charleston progressing NE'ward near \nCape Fear and approaching the CWA. There is uncertainty if the \nstorms near Charleston keep their structure through time due we \nhave CIN developing with the light rain spreading over ENC right\nnow and the shortwave over SC weakening as it approaches NC. \n\nWith the more progressive nature of the scattered line moving\nthrough ENC and lighter, stratiform rain behind it, flash \nflooding potential has gone down drastically in the next 3 \nhours. While the environment is very favorable with PWATs over\n2.2\" and long, skinny CAPEs, the lack of substantial forcing and\nshear has thrown a wrench in the flooding potential. Have \ndecided to keep the Flood Watch in effect until 5 PM despite \nthe reduced potential, as it will only take one boundary in a \nvery messy regime to set up shop for torrential downpours and \nlocalized flash flooding to occur. Have trended down in QPFs \nwith the decreased potential with this update, from 1-3\" in the \nflood watch area, to 1-1.5 inches. OBX from Oregon Inlet down to\nOcracoke has the benefit of being in closer proximity to the \ngulf stream convection, so if any outflow or boundary shifts \nwestward over OBX, that could be a focus for persistent \nconvection later today.\n\nThis afternoon through mid-week there continue to be be daily \nchances for heavy showers and thunderstorms. Seeing how this\nmorning is playing out, it seems like any additional shortwaves\nalong a weakening trough may not be enough to provide enough\nstorm coverage for flooding potential. However, as the trough\nweakens in the coming days the sea breeze may become the \ndominant forcing mechanism, and be the focus for minor flooding\npotential going forward through mid-week. \n\nWe remain in a Slight (level 2/5) Excessive Rain Outlook for \ntoday with best chances at seeing flash flooding likely along \nHwy 17 and points east. We are in a Marginal (level 1/5) ERO for\nTuesday for the entirety of eastern NC.\n\nBy the end of the week it looks like we return back to climo or\nbelow climo PoPs as a lack of mid level shortwaves or \nsignificant forcing will be noted across ENC though we do begin \nto warm back up with temps returning to the upper 80s to low 90s\ninland and mid 80s along the OBX.",
